{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfaH6gX2w56Nf6UYi8GRX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 02 is done. Every pebbling statement recorded in iteration 1 holds on every small input checked, with no counterexamples. The gate passes: `lake build` succeeds, the only `sorry` is the existing one (limit 1), and pytest shows 1251 passed, 2 skipped, 1 expected failure.\n\n**What was built** (in `paper2/complex_check.py`):\n- **Games:** four pebbling games, written from the sources' own wording. Each is decided exactly by searching every position that stays within a pebble budget, and each result is confirmed by showing one pebble fewer fails.\n  - progressive black-white, under Lengauer's rules (turning a white pebble black is optional) and under Kirousis & Papadimitriou's (turning is automatic);\n  - progressive black;\n  - black-white and black with re-pebbling allowed.\n- **Graph constructions:** Lengauer's two graphs built from a dag (`lengauer_u`, `lengauer_d`), the matrix whose MOSP value should equal the pebbling number (`pebble_matrix`), all acyclic orientations of a graph, and a chordality test.\n- **How to run it:** `python -m paper2.complex_check --pebbling` takes 279 s on 32 cores and writes `paper2/data/pebbling_check.json`.\n\n**What was checked:**\n- every dag on up to 6 vertices, plus 20,000 random dags on 7;\n- every small graph from the networkx atlas (at most 7 vertices) with vertices plus edges ≤ 14, for Lengauer's Theorem 3 (299 graphs);\n- every graph on up to 7 vertices, over all its orientations (1,252 graphs);\n- every rooted tree on up to 11 vertices, with arcs pointing both towards and away from the root (4,394);\n- 359,744 play positions for the \"no recontamination\" lemma behind Kirousis & Papadimitriou's proof. It holds in every play that can still finish, not only in optimal ones.\n\n**Findings:**\n- **Lengauer's remark that re-pebbling doesn't help on trees fails earlier than we thought.** For trees directed away from the root, the smallest counterexample has 7 vertices, not 13 (Kirousis & Papadimitriou's ternary tree). It is a root with three legs of length 2: progressive pebbling needs 3 pebbles, with re-pebbling 2 suffice. No such tree on 6 or fewer vertices fails. Up to 11 vertices the gap is never more than 1.\n- **On trees directed towards the root, re-pebbling never helped** in any of the 4,394 cases, in either game. That supports reading Lengauer's remark as being about those trees.\n- **The gap between progressive and unrestricted pebbling grows.** On Lengauer's dag built from the complete graph Kₙ, progressive pebbling needs n + 1 pebbles for n = 2, 3, 4, while unrestricted pebbling needs 3 every time.\n\nThe results are written up as a new section P.7 at the end of `paper2/equivalences.md`, and earlier sections now point to it. The tests are in `tests/test_pebbling_check.py` (20 tests on hand-checked dags plus a quick full run). The only error found was one of my own hand-computed test values; the solver was right.\n\n**Limits and what's left:**\n- Dags are exhaustive only up to 6 vertices, and the node-search comparison covers graphs up to 6 vertices.\n- I did not update `paper2/problem_transformations.md`; the new problem section and its proofs belong with Lean items 03–04.\n\nThe item is marked `[x]` in `iterations.md`, PROGRESS.md is updated to 2/13, and nothing was committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1908,"cache_read_input_tokens":126070,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1908,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":767,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80ccdd8d-3d4d-4254-b7aa-19506c88dbca","uuid":"c08cd09c-cd35-47fb-96b8-842984ce8c42","timestamp":"2026-09-3